From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix: sanitize GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE atom

The arm64 Steam client does not write the vector this atom is documented to
carry. Measured on hardware by sweeping each UI control and watching the atom:
the "Primary Hue" slider moves NOTHING, the "Peak Saturation" slider drives
vec[1] (sweeping 0.5..1.0), and the night-mode tint slider drives vec[0]. So the
client sends [amount, saturation] and never sends a hue at all; vec[2] is left
as uninitialised garbage.

gamescope renders HSV(hue, saturation*amount, 1.0) faithfully, so reading vec[1]
as the hue turns night mode into a magenta/red screen instead of warm amber.
Reading vec[2] as the saturation is worse than wrong, it is a coin flip: the
garbage is re-rolled per install, and a large positive (1.02e15, 6.5e22) clamps
to 1.0 and works by luck while a negative (-6.25527e-27) clamps to 0.0, which
multiplies the tint away so night mode does nothing at all at any intensity.
That is why "night mode does nothing" was never reproducible across cards.

This was first read as a consequence of this panel exposing no EDID, but other
arm64 systems on unrelated panels hit the identical vector, so the cause is the
client and not our display stack. Worth stating because it rules out the
"advertise real colorimetry via a kernel panel EDID" route a second time: even a
perfect EDID would not change what the client writes into this atom.

Take the two fields the client really sends, pin the hue it never sends to
amber, and guard both against the non-finite values this client is known to
emit. Night mode becomes a reliable warm cast whose strength follows the tint
slider and whose intensity follows the saturation slider. "Primary Hue" stays
inert because the client transmits nothing for it.
---
 src/steamcompmgr.cpp | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6349,6 +6349,26 @@
 		nightmode.hue = vec[1];
 		nightmode.saturation = vec[2];
 
+		// novadeck: re-read the atom the way the arm64 Steam client actually fills it.
+		// HW-measured by sweeping each UI control: "Primary Hue" moves nothing, "Peak
+		// Saturation" drives vec[1] (0.5..1.0), the tint slider drives vec[0]. The client
+		// sends [amount, saturation] and no hue; vec[2] is uninitialised garbage, re-rolled
+		// per install (1.02e15 and 6.5e22 seen, and -6.25527e-27 on 2026-08-04).
+		//
+		// So the stock decode is wrong twice: vec[1] as a hue gives a magenta/red screen, and
+		// vec[2] as a saturation is a coin flip -- a big positive clamps to 1.0 and works by
+		// luck, a negative clamps to 0.0 and multiplies the tint away, leaving night mode
+		// visibly dead at every intensity. Hence: take vec[0]/vec[1], pin the hue, and guard
+		// both against the non-finite values this client is known to emit.
+		{
+			constexpr float kNightModeAmberHue = 25.0f / 360.0f;  // 25deg amber, matches Steam Deck night mode
+			const float flAmount     = vec[0];
+			const float flSaturation = vec[1];
+			nightmode.amount     = std::isfinite( flAmount )     ? std::clamp( flAmount,     0.0f, 1.0f ) : 0.0f;
+			nightmode.saturation = std::isfinite( flSaturation ) ? std::clamp( flSaturation, 0.0f, 1.0f ) : 1.0f;
+			nightmode.hue        = kNightModeAmberHue;
+		}
+
 		if ( set_color_nightmode( nightmode ) )
 			hasRepaint = true;
 	}
--
